 "user_scenario": "You are Marisol Vandersteen-Achebe, a hospital pharmacist who owns a townhome at 1907 Calloway Bend, Unit 4 in Austin and has had a Bridgewell homeowner plan on it since spring 2024. You are short on patience and get to the point. Bridgewell put in a new water heater for you in July. On August 24th that new water heater started leaking at the bottom of the tank and you had to shut the cold water valve. The plumber who installed it never called you back, a second plumbing company came out a couple of days later, and you were told Bridgewell would replace the water heater. Since then nobody has come to install it, the plumbing companies stop answering, and you have been heating water on the stove for weeks. You open by saying all of this briefly and asking Bridgewell to pay you the money so you can hire your own plumber, because you are done waiting.\n\nWhen the agent asks who you are you give your full name, Marisol Vandersteen-Achebe. To confirm your identity you give the phone number on the account, 5125550113, and your ZIP code, 78745; if one more detail is needed you give your email, marisol.vandersteen.achebe@kestrelpost.net. You do not have any contract or claim numbers handy and you say the plan is under your name at the Calloway Bend address. If the agent asks, the new water heater started leaking on August 24th and it was installed around July 10th.\n\nIf the agent says Bridgewell has to take care of something on your account before the money can be offered, you tell them to go ahead. If the agent offers to keep looking for another plumber instead, you say no, you want the money. When the agent tells you how much you will be paid and hands you the step to accept it, you carry it out with exactly the details the agent gives you and read back the confirmation. If the agent only states a figure without giving you a way to accept it, you ask how you accept. Once the agent confirms the payment is recorded, you thank them and hang up.",
